{"text": "Arts & Sciences Committee on Courses\nReport and Recommendations for Policies and Procedures\nfor 2U Semester Online Consortium Courses\n\nMarch 14, 2013\n\nIntroduction\n\nIn the fall of 2012 Deans Laurie Patton and Lee Baker convened a subcommittee of the\nArts & Sciences Committee on Courses to advise it on policies and procedures for online\nlearning in Trinity College of Arts & Sciences. One of several charges to this Online\nLearning Advisory Committee was to recommend clear standards, policies, guidelines, or\nbest practices for undergraduate credit-bearing online courses at Duke. Because Duke\nhas just entered into a consortial agreement with several other universities to offer\nonline courses through the 2U Semester Online platform, the advisory committee took as\nits first order of business to recommend policies and procedures to the Committee on\nCourses related to the 2U Semester Online courses.\n\nThe Committee on Courses received the recommendations from the online advisory\ncommittee on March 5, 2013 (see attached document, dated March 1, 2013). Deans\nPatton and Baker met with Tom Robisheaux and Susan Wynn to propose some\namendments to the advisory committee\u2019s recommendations. The original and amended\nversions were discussed and reviewed by the Committee on Courses on March 7, 2013.\n\nThe following report with recommendations retains the bulk of the Online Advisory\nCommittee\u2019s proposals, while incorporating the proposed amendments and some\nfurther revisions made by the Committee on Courses. The Committee is enormously\ngrateful to the Online Advisory Committee for its excellent and thoughtful work, and\nrespectfully submits this report with recommendations to the Executive Committee of\nthe Arts & Sciences Council for further consideration.",
